Z||000004||MinnGreenGT||10-17-2001||09:36 AM||||Fran's Indy GT has matching white lace wheels (don't think they're stock though). I think it'd look great- go for it!||||reg||1
Z||000005||ChopTop||10-17-2001||10:29 AM||||I would think it would look good as long as you use the same shade of white you car is.  Keeping them clean would be my main concern, brake dust can build up rather quickly.  I'd purchase plastic brake dust shields to help keep them cleaner.  You may want to consider powder coating but I'd ask the business if they offer a warranty.  I've been told that durability largely depends on the materials and methods used.||||reg||1
Z||000006||K2m4||10-17-2001||11:44 AM||||My 86 Gt has white factory rims ...looks cool as hell with them like that<P>kerm<BR>86 GT<BR>86 2M4<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fformula88||10-17-2001||12:16 PM||||Pontiac has made a number of white cars with white wheels to match, and so far they have all looked fine to me.  One that comes readily to mind is the last generation Grand Am GT's.  I have also seen Grand Prix's with white wheels.  I think the Fiero Indy Wheels look great!<P>I think you could do a good job on those ground FX with a rattle can.... just gotta know how to finish it off.  I'd recommend using a base coat/clear coat white paint.  You have to sand it smooth and buff it in the end.  It may not last 10 years because the quality of the pain isn't the best (unless you get some paint made specifically for the car and put into a rattle can.  Its a better quality paint, a bit more pricey too!), but I have had rattle can paint last a long time on spot repairs I have done.<P>I also think the honeycomb wheels would look very good white!  You might want to see how much it would cost to have them powder coated white.  I think it would hold up a little better on the wheels.||||reg||1
Z||000008||MinnGreenGT||10-17-2001||12:26 PM||||Had to dig through my huge folder of images to find this one... it's Fran's Turbo V6/Indy/GT.  I love that car.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fran's_pace_car_arrivingM_1.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by MinnGreenGT (edited 10-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Old Lar||10-17-2001||12:44 PM||||Alex Maxim has a white 88GT produced late in the production run.  It came stock with white wheels, supposedly a color that was to be offered in 1989 and Pontiac was using up all remaining components in their Fiero inventory.<P>Frans rims came from England. They are 17" rims and very expensive ones at that.||||reg||1
Z||000010||mrfiero||10-17-2001||03:21 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Old Lar:<BR><B>Alex Maxim has a white 88GT produced late in the production run.  It came stock with white wheels, supposedly a color that was to be offered in 1989 and Pontiac was using up all remaining components in their Fiero inventory.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I've heard this story and seen the car, but still feel that this is another questionable thing.  I personally don't believe that any Fiero (other than the Indy) came from the factory with white wheels.  No one at Pontiac has ever acknowledged it, so (at least for me) until they do I don't buy it.<P>This is similar to a guy in the Denver area many years ago selling his '87 GT "prototype".  He claimed it was a GM prototype and hade a supercharged 2.8.......I never saw it in person, but debunked it because GM has never sold any Fiero prototypes, so how did he manage to get one?<P>I suppose I would believe the white wheel thing if there was a picture of it being built in the factory with the workers installing them on the car (fat chance, I know).  If I remember correctly the RPO code on his car says it came with black rims.<P><BR>||||reg||1
